\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl276\slmult1\kerning2\f0\fs24 TRANSFORMER \par
\lang16393 A \b Transformer\b0  is a deep learning architecture introduced in the 2017 paper \i "Attention Is All You Need"\i0  that revolutionized Natural Language Processing (NLP) and later became the foundation of modern Large Language Models (LLMs) such as GPT, BERT, Llama, Claude, and Gemini. Unlike earlier sequence models such as Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) and Long Short-Term Memory networks (LSTMs), which process words one at a time, a Transformer processes an entire sequence simultaneously. This parallel processing allows it to train much faster and capture long-range dependencies between words more effectively. The core idea behind the Transformer is the \b attention mechanism\b0 , which enables every word in a sentence to dynamically determine which other words are important for understanding its meaning. For example, in the sentence \i "The animal didn't cross the street because it was tired,"\i0  the model learns that the word \i "it"\i0  refers to \i "animal"\i0  by assigning higher attention to that word. This ability to selectively focus on relevant information is what makes Transformers extraordinarily powerful.\par
\lang1033 WORKING:\par
\lang16393 The Transformer workflow begins when input text is broken into smaller units called \b tokens\b0 . Each token is converted into a numerical vector through an \b embedding layer\b0 , creating a mathematical representation of the text. Since Transformers process all tokens simultaneously, they need a way to understand word order. This is achieved using \b Positional Encoding\b0 , which injects information about each token's position in the sequence. The resulting vectors are then passed through a series of Transformer layers. Inside each layer, the model generates three vectors for every token: a \b Query (Q)\b0  vector that asks what information the token is looking for, a \b Key (K)\b0  vector that describes what information the token contains, and a \b Value (V)\b0  vector that stores the actual information. Attention scores are computed by comparing Queries with Keys, determining how strongly each token should attend to every other token. These scores are normalized using the Softmax function and then used to weight the Value vectors, producing a new context-aware representation of each token.\par
To increase learning capacity, Transformers use \b Multi-Head Attention\b0 , where several attention mechanisms operate in parallel. Each head learns different linguistic relationships such as grammar, semantic meaning, entity references, or contextual dependencies. The outputs from all attention heads are combined and passed through a \b Feed-Forward Neural Network (FFN)\b0  that performs additional transformations. Throughout the architecture, \b Residual Connections\b0  and \b Layer Normalization\b0  are employed to stabilize training and prevent information loss as data passes through many layers. In the original Transformer architecture, the \b Encoder\b0  stack creates rich contextual representations of the input sequence, while the \b Decoder\b0  stack generates output tokens one at a time using both self-attention and encoder-decoder attention. Modern generative models like GPT use only the decoder portion, whereas models such as BERT primarily use encoder layers.\par
One of the most important reasons Transformers outperform previous architectures is that they can model relationships between words regardless of their distance in a sentence. Traditional RNNs struggle to remember information from earlier parts of a sequence because information must travel through many intermediate steps. Transformers eliminate this bottleneck by allowing direct interaction between all tokens through self-attention. This capability scales effectively to billions of parameters and massive datasets, enabling modern AI systems to understand context, answer questions, translate languages, summarize documents, write code, generate images, and perform complex reasoning tasks. As a result, the Transformer architecture has become the fundamental building block behind nearly every state-of-the-art AI model developed today.\par
