\b Pretraining vs Fine-Tuning in Transformers\par
\b0 Modern Transformer-based models such as GPT, BERT, Llama, and T5 are typically developed in \b two major phases: Pretraining and Fine-Tuning\b0 . These phases allow a model to first learn general knowledge about language and then specialize in specific tasks.\par
\par
\b 1. Pretraining\par
Pretraining\b0  is the initial learning phase where a Transformer is trained on massive amounts of unlabeled data, often consisting of books, websites, articles, research papers, and code repositories. During this stage, the model learns the fundamental structure of language, grammar, reasoning patterns, facts, semantic relationships, and contextual understanding.\par
The objective is not to teach the model a specific task but rather to help it develop a broad understanding of how language works. For example, GPT models are typically trained using a \b next-token prediction\b0  objective, where the model predicts the next word in a sequence:\par
Input: "Artificial Intelligence is"\par
Target: "transforming"\par
By repeating this prediction billions of times across huge datasets, the model gradually learns:\par

